\newlabel{fig:CorchiaBayes}{{\rEfLiNK{x1-4010r4}{\csname :autoref\endcsname{document}4}}{\rEfLiNK{x1-4010r4}{\csname :autoref\endcsname{document}14}}{\rEfLiNK{x1-4010r4}{\csname :autoref\endcsname{document}The same data as Figure\leavevmode \special {t4ht@+&{35}x00A0{59}}x{}\let \prOteCt \relax \let \prOteCt \relax \Protect \::ref {fig:CorchiaMC}, but using the Bayesian approach. y-axes display the prior probability (a), likelihood (b) and posterior probabilities (c and d), respectively. For this relatively young sample, the Bayesian method yields similar results to the `Monte Carlo' solution.\vspace  \medskipamount \relax }}{document.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:AV03Bayes}{{\rEfLiNK{x1-4012r5}{\csname :autoref\endcsname{document}5}}{\rEfLiNK{x1-4012r5}{\csname :autoref\endcsname{document}14}}{\rEfLiNK{x1-4012r5}{\csname :autoref\endcsname{document}Posterior distributions of $[4/8]_i$ and $t$ for sample AV03 of \citet  {pickering2019}. Panels\leavevmode \special {t4ht@+&{35}x00A0{59}}x{}a and b represent the original U--Pb data of Figure\leavevmode \special {t4ht@+&{35}x00A0{59}}x{}\let \prOteCt \relax \let \prOteCt \relax \Protect \::ref {fig:AV03MC1}, whereas panels\leavevmode \special {t4ht@+&{35}x00A0{59}}x{}c and d show the modified data of Figure\leavevmode \special {t4ht@+&{35}x00A0{59}}x{}\let \prOteCt \relax \let \prOteCt \relax \Protect \::ref {fig:AV03MC2}. Likelihood functions are provided in panels\leavevmode \special {t4ht@+&{35}x00A0{59}}x{}b of the latter two figures. A uniform prior was used but is not shown. The modes of the posterior distributions agree with the modes of the `Monte Carlo' solutions. However, whereas the `Monte Carlo' algorithm suggests a high degree of confidence in the disequilibrium correction, the Bayesian approach shows that one cannot rule out a much higher age of the sample, including the uncorrected date of 5.6\,Ma.\vspace  \medskipamount \relax }}{document.1}{}}
